    {
      "sample_index": 5,
      "text": "To find the mean value of the measures of the three interior angles of any triangle, we start by recalling that the sum of the interior angles of a triangle is always 180 degrees. This is a fundamental property of triangles in Euclidean geometry.\n\nLet's denote the measures of the three interior angles of the triangle as \\(A\\), \\(B\\), and \\(C\\). According to the property mentioned, we have:\n\\[ A + B + C = 180^\\circ. \\]\n\nThe mean value of the measures of the three angles is calculated by dividing the sum of the angles by the number of angles, which is 3. Therefore, the mean value is:\n\\[ \\text{Mean} = \\frac{A + B + C}{3}. \\]\n\nSubstituting the sum of the angles into the equation, we get:\n\\[ \\text{Mean} = \\frac{180^\\circ}{3} = 60^\\circ. \\]\n\nThus, the mean value of the measures of the three interior angles of any triangle is \\(\\boxed{60}\\).",
